#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Zenko - a free pixel-art focus companion app for Windows.

A little fox lives at the side of your screen. Start a focus session and it watches
which window you are in. Open YouTube / Instagram / Reddit / games and it gets
cross and tells you to stop procrastinating.

Needs only Python 3.8+ for Windows (tkinter ships with it). No pip installs.
Run:   pythonw zenko.py        (or double-click start_zenko.bat)

The main window (sidebar: Home, Timetable, Chat, Settings) opens at start; closing it leaves the fox on your desktop.
Controls:  double-click fox = new focus session   |   right-click = menu
           middle-click     = open chat           |   drag = move the fox
           click = pet the fox
The fox's belly shows your focus/break timer (or the clock when idle) and its face follows
your mouse. It reminds you to take breaks and, in a session, closes a distraction after a minute.
Settings live in  %APPDATA%\\FoxBuddy\\config.json  (right-click > Edit settings).

Privacy: everything stays on your PC. The optional "smart brain" talks only to Ollama running
on this same computer (127.0.0.1). The fox touches the internet ONLY to look something up: when you ask
(weather, search, news, currency, time in a city) or, if "search when she doesn't know" is on, when you
ask her a question she can't answer. Only your question is sent to the search engine, then she reads the
top pages. Switch that off any time: right-click > Internet lookups, or Settings.
What she learns (your habits, your shorthand, things she looked up) stays in learned.json on this PC and
"Forget everything" on the You page wipes it.
"""
